"},{\"id\":\"277\",\"title\":\"WWII: German Surrender: Signing of the Instrument of Surrender\",\"description\":\"Under the direction of the new German leader, Admiral Karl Donitz, General Alfred Jodl signed the Instrument of Surrender on May 7, 1945, at Reims, France.\",\"url\":\"https://www.history.navy.mil/content/history/museums/nmusn/explore/photography/wwii/wwii-europe/german-surrender/signing-instrument.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"278\",\"title\":\"Decision to Drop the Atomic Bomb | Harry S. Truman\",\"description\":\"The Japanese bombing of Pearl Harbor on December 7, 1941, brought the United States officially into World War II. In the surprise attack, Japan sunk several ...Missing:  effects | Show results with:effects\",\"url\":\"https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/education/presidential-inquiries/decision-drop-atomic-bomb\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"279\",\"title\":\"Atomic Bomb: August 6, 1945 | Harry S. Truman\",\"description\":\"After Japanese leaders flatly rejected the Potsdam Declaration, President Truman authorized use of the atomic bomb anytime after August 3, 1945. On the clear ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.trumanlibrary.gov/education/lesson-plans/atomic-bomb-august-6-1945\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"280\",\"title\":\"Little Boy - Hiroshima - August 6, 1945\",\"description\":\"The bomb weighed 9,000 pounds and had a diameter of only 28 inches. The gun-type weapon possessed the power of 26,000,000 pounds of high explosives. Nuclear ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.history.navy.mil/content/history/museums/nmusn/explore/photography/wwii/wwii-pacific/bombardment-japan/bombs-atomic/little-boy-hiroshima.html\",\"favicon\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"281\",\"title\":\"\\\"Little Boy\\\" Atomic Bomb - Air Force Museum\",\"description\":\"\\\"Little Boy\\\" was the first nuclear weapon used in warfare, detonated over Hiroshima, Japan, on Aug. 6, 1945, and was a gun-type weapon.\",\"url\":\"https://www.nationalmuseum.af.mil/Visit/Museum-Exhibits/Fact-Sheets/Display/Artic